<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/foundational/memory">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Try to remember your last birthday. Whatever surfaces — a cake, a face, a fragment of song — did not
    arrive in your mind like a video playing back. Somewhere in your brain, a physical trace was built when
    the day happened, stored for months, and reassembled just now into something that feels like a memory.
    Understanding how that building, storing and rebuilding works is the whole business of memory research,
    and it matters far beyond the laboratory. It explains why cramming fails, why confident eyewitnesses can
    still be wrong, why doodling during a boring phone call can be smart, and why a person who cannot form
    new memories can still learn a new skill.
  </p>
  <p>
    The founding insight of the <strong>cognitive approach</strong> — the school of psychology this lesson
    belongs to — is that between the world coming in and behaviour coming out, something processes
    information. Cognitive psychologists treat the mind as an information-processing system: like a computer,
    it takes input (light, sound, touch), encodes it, stores it, transforms it and retrieves it. Because the
    processing is internal, it cannot be watched directly. Instead, researchers <em>infer</em> it: they measure
    behaviour carefully — how much is recalled, how fast a decision is made, which errors people make — and
    design experiments that let one explanation win over another. The approach also assumes the mind uses
    <strong>schemas</strong>: organised packages of prior knowledge that shape what we notice and how we
    interpret it. Those assumptions drive everything that follows.
  </p>
  <p>
    Memory itself is not one thing but a chain of <strong>three systems in series</strong>. The
    <strong>sensory store</strong> holds raw impressions for under a second — a flash, an echo. What you pay
    attention to moves into <strong>working memory</strong>, the conscious workspace that keeps a handful of
    items alive for 15–30 seconds unless you keep rehearsing them. What gets elaborated and connected can then
    be filed into <strong>long-term memory</strong>, whose capacity is, as far as anyone has been able to
    measure, effectively unlimited — measured not in megabytes but in years. This chain is the
    <strong>multi-store model</strong> of Atkinson and Shiffrin (1968), and it remains the map on which almost
    every memory finding gets pinned.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Ebbinghaus's forgetting curve — raise the strength slider (more review, better sleep) and watch decay flatten" widget="plotter" config={forgetCfg} />

  <h2>Working memory: a desk, not a box</h2>
  <p>
    The multi-store model treated short-term memory as a passive bucket with about seven slots. In 1956,
    George Miller noticed a strange regularity in everyday cognition: people can hold roughly
    <strong>7 ± 2 items</strong> in mind at once — seven digits, seven words, seven objects. But later work
    showed the bucket leaks and misbehaves in ways a single box cannot explain. Someone can simultaneously
    repeat a phone number <em>and</em> picture a route home, yet the same person cannot listen to two people
    talking at once. If short-term memory were one store, all tasks would compete for it equally. They don't.
  </p>
  <p>
    Baddeley and Hitch (1974) therefore split the workspace into a <strong>working memory</strong> with
    parts: a <em>phonological loop</em> that rehearses sound and speech (the inner voice), a
    <em>visuospatial sketchpad</em> that manipulates images and spatial layouts (the inner eye), and a
    <em>central executive</em> — the attention manager that decides what the two loops work on. A fourth
    component, the <em>episodic buffer</em>, was added in 2000 to bind the streams together into single
    episodes that can be handed to long-term memory. The model explains the everyday juggling act: you can
    hum a tune while reading a map (different stores), but you cannot read and hold a conversation (both
    compete for one).
  </p>
  <p>
    Jackie Andrade's <strong>doodling study</strong> (2009) turned this architecture into a practical
    surprise. Participants listened to a deliberately dull mock telephone message listing who would attend a
    party; one group was told to shade printed shapes (doodle) while listening, the other just listened.
    On a surprise memory test, doodlers recalled about 29% more — roughly 7.5 of the names and places versus
    5.8 for the listeners — and were better at spotting the names of people who would <em>not</em> attend.
    The mechanism is exactly what working-memory theory predicts: a boring listening task leaves spare
    capacity in the sketchpad, which the mind fills with daydreaming that competes with the message.
    Doodling occupies just enough of the sketchpad to block the daydream without overloading the loop.
    Attention, not effort, is the bottleneck — which is the central executive's lesson in action.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> doodling is not multitasking that "uses up" attention. It works
  <em>because</em> it occupies an under-used store — the sketchpad — leaving the verbal loop free. Genuinely
  competing verbal tasks (texting while listening) impair recall badly; the benefit is specific to tasks
  that tap a different subsystem.</p>

  <h2>Forgetting: the curve, and what fights it</h2>
  <p>
    Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885) did memory research the hard way: as his own only participant, learning lists
    of meaningless syllables because they came pre-loaded with no meaning to confuse things. Re-testing
    himself across hours and days, he traced the shape that almost every memory curve has followed since:
    loss is steepest in the first hours, then flattens into a long tail. His data fit what is now usually
    drawn as exponential decay — retention <strong>R = e<sup>−t/s</sup></strong>, where <em>t</em> is time
    since study and <em>s</em> is a "memory strength" constant. The widget above lets you control that
    strength directly: with s = 1 day, retention has collapsed before breakfast on day two; with s = 30 days,
    over half survives a month. Every study technique that works is secretly fighting for a bigger <em>s</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two strongest fighters are well replicated. <strong>Spacing beats massing</strong>: three one-hour
    sessions spread across a week beat a single three-hour block, because each new encounter arrives when the
    trace has partly decayed, and the extra effort to retrieve it strengthens the trace more (the spacing
    effect, one of psychology's most reliable findings). <strong>Testing is studying</strong>: actively
    retrieving an answer — flashcards, closing the book and reconstructing — strengthens memory far more than
    re-reading, which only builds a comfortable feeling of familiarity. And <strong>sleep is part of
    studying</strong>: consolidation, the physical process of stabilising a new trace in long-term store,
    happens largely during sleep, which is why all-nighters mostly delete the day's work. Chunking helps a
    different bottleneck — packing items together so seven slots hold more (expert chess players remember
    whole boards, not 64 squares; Chase and Simon, 1973).
  </p>

  <h2>Memory is reconstructive: eyewitnesses and line-ups</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the uncomfortable part: retrieval does not replay a recording; it rebuilds. Frederic Bartlett
    (1932) showed that people rewrite stories to fit their schemas, dropping what does not fit and
    sharpening what does. Elizabeth Loftus and John Palmer (1974) showed the rewriting happening in real
    time: participants who watched a car crash and were asked how fast the cars were going when they
    <em>smashed</em> into each other estimated higher speeds than those asked about cars that
    <em>hit</em> — and a week later, a third of the "smashed" group remembered broken glass that was never
    there. Every retrieval slightly rewrites the trace, so confident testimony can be confidently wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is why police line-ups are an experiment about memory, and Joanna Pozzulo's <strong>line-up
    studies</strong> are core syllabus material. Pozzulo and colleagues had children (roughly 5–14) and
    adults watch a video of a staged theft, then try to identify the culprit from a line-up. When the real
    culprit was present, adults were somewhat more accurate than children. The dramatic result came from
    line-ups where the culprit was <em>absent</em>: young children chose someone anyway far more often than
    adults did — they picked the look-alike rather than saying "he's not here". Children, it seems, treat the
    task as "pick the person who looks most like the thief", while adults are more willing to answer "not
    present". Pozzulo's practical response — teaching children to consider rejection as a real option, and
    comparing simultaneous line-ups with sequential ones — has fed directly into recommendations for real
    interviews with child witnesses. The stakes are enormous: mistaken identification is a leading cause of
    wrongful convictions.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the confident witness is the accurate witness. The research says
  confidence correlates poorly with accuracy and can be inflated by post-event suggestion. What predicts
  accuracy is viewing conditions, delay and the absence of leading questions — not certainty.</p>

  <h2>Minds reading minds</h2>
  <p>
    Memory stores the past; cognition also reads other people's present. Understanding that others have
    beliefs, intentions and feelings different from your own — <strong>theory of mind</strong> — is a
    cognitive skill in its own right, and Simon Baron-Cohen and colleagues measured it with the
    <strong>"Reading the Mind in the Eyes" test</strong> (2001): 36 photographs of the eye region alone,
    with four mental-state words (e.g. <em>sceptical, playful, irritated, bored</em>) to choose from for
    each. Adults with Asperger syndrome or high-functioning autism scored significantly lower than matched
    controls — typically several points below the control mean of about 26 out of 36 — even though their
    general intelligence was normal, and women scored slightly higher than men across groups. The finding
    localised social-cognitive difficulty to reading others from minimal cues, and the test is now used
    widely (and carefully, since eye-region photos differ across cultures) as a measure of cognitive empathy.
    It sits naturally in this lesson because it shows the cognitive method at work: infer an internal
    process — here, mentalising — from performance on a carefully designed task.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    A few layers below the model: <strong>levels of processing</strong> (Craik and Lockhart, 1972) argues
    that stores matter less than depth of encoding — judging whether <em>cra</em> rhymes with <em>cot</em>
    produces far worse recall than judging whether it fits "We saw a crazy cat", even though rehearsal time
    is identical. Meaning, not repetition, builds <em>s</em>. <strong>Encoding specificity</strong> (Tulving)
    adds that retrieval succeeds when cues match the encoding context, which is why recalling a fact in the
    exam hall can feel harder than recalling it in the library where you read it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The classic case of H.M., whose hippocampus was removed in 1953 to treat epilepsy, revealed the systems'
    wiring: he lost the ability to form new conscious memories, yet could still learn mirror-drawing skills
    day after day with no memory of practising. Long-term memory, it turns out, is not a single system:
    <em>episodic</em> memory for events, <em>semantic</em> memory for facts, and <em>procedural</em> memory
    for skills live in different circuits, and consolidation runs on repeating ripples of hippocampal
    activity during sleep — the mechanism behind "sleep is part of studying". At the cellular level,
    long-term potentiation (LTP) — a synapse that is repeatedly co-activated becomes permanently easier to
    trigger — is the leading candidate for what a memory physically is. And at the frontier, researchers ask
    whether each reconsolidation window (the minutes after retrieval when a trace is writable again) could be
    used to weaken traumatic memories. The 1968 model is still the map; the expedition is far from over.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ebbinghaus (1885); Miller (1956); Atkinson & Shiffrin (1968); Baddeley & Hitch (1974); Chase & Simon (1973); Craik & Lockhart (1972); Bartlett (1932); Loftus & Palmer (1974); Andrade (2009); Pozzulo & Lindsay (1998); Baron-Cohen et al. (2001).</p>
